<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!-- Review reminders. POST_NOTIFICATIONS is runtime-requested on Android 13+
         (see ReviewNotificationService.requestPermission), deliberately at the
         moment a review clock actually starts rather than on cold start.
         RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED lets pending reminders survive a reboot.
         SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM is intentionally NOT declared: Play restricts it,
         and a study reminder does not justify exact-alarm privileges. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED"/>

    <!-- Android Auto Backup, declared on purpose rather than defaulted.
         Decided 2026-08-15, during the pre-Play security audit.

         `allowBackup` defaults to **true**, so this line changes no behaviour at
         all. It is here because "true by inheritance" and "true on purpose" are
         indistinguishable in a diff, and this flag decides whether a student's
         entire exam history leaves the phone.

         What it means: the SQLite record (`app_flutter/sahm.sqlite`) and the
         preferences beside it are uploaded to **the student's own Google
         account** — encrypted with their device credential, which Google itself
         cannot read on Android 9+ — and restored automatically onto their next
         phone.

         Why it is still true, and why that is an open question rather than a
         settled one. The first draft of this comment argued that Ather's own
         cloud backup is opt-in, so Auto Backup was the only thing standing
         between a student who never enabled it and a lost phone. **That was
         wrong.** `BackupSyncNotifier` uploads on a 20-second debounce after any
         write and again when the app backgrounds, for every signed-in student,
         and the auth gate makes signing in mandatory — so every student already
         has an automatic Firestore copy, and `BackupSyncGate._restoreIfEmpty`
         pulls it onto a new phone unaided.

         Auto Backup also only runs on an **unmetered** network, so it does not
         rescue the offline-only student that argument was built around either.
         Both paths need the same connection.

         What is left is the narrow case where Firestore cannot take the record
         but Auto Backup could — most concretely a student whose compressed
         record passes `_maxBlobBytes` and fails with `BackupFailure.tooLarge`,
         who then has no Ather-side cloud copy at all. That is real, and it is
         an argument for raising or paging that ceiling rather than for keeping
         a second uncontrolled copy of a minor's exam history somewhere else.

         So the audit's corrected recommendation is **false**, and this is
         deliberately left at true: flipping it removes a recovery path, however
         narrow, and CLAUDE.md Rule 1 says that is not a change to make casually
         or on an auditor's say-so. It is the app owner's call. If it flips, it is
         a one-line change here and a line in the Data safety form.

         **The database and the preferences are one unit — never back up one
         without the other.** `app_flutter/sahm.sqlite` holds the record and
         `shared_prefs` holds `last_account_uid`, and §3.30's "the device belongs
         to one account at a time" wipe compares an incoming uid against that
         preference. Restore the database without the preference and `previous`
         reads null, the wipe never fires, and the *second* student to sign in on
         a restored phone opens the *first* student's curriculum. Excluding
         `shared_prefs` for privacy would look like a tightening and would
         actually reintroduce the exact data-mixing bug §3.30 was written to fix.

         Not done, and deliberately left as a follow-up: an `<include>` allowlist
         that backs up the study data while excluding Firebase's cached auth
         token, so a restored phone asks for a sign-in instead of arriving already
         signed in. That is a better posture, but an allowlist that names the
         wrong backup domain silently drops the database from every backup, and
         verifying it needs a real backup → wipe → restore cycle on a device we
         are free to erase. Do not add it without running that cycle. -->
    <application
        android:label="Ather"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!-- Load-bearing, and easy to miss: since flutter_local_notifications 16
             the plugin's own manifest declares **permissions only** — verified
             against the copy in the pub cache, which contains nothing but
             POST_NOTIFICATIONS and VIBRATE. The two receivers below are what
             actually turn a scheduled alarm into a notification, so without them
             `zonedSchedule` succeeds, the alarm fires, and nothing is ever shown.
             That is a silent failure with no error anywhere, which is exactly how
             it went unnoticed.

             ScheduledNotificationReceiver  -> shows a scheduled reminder.
             ScheduledNotificationBootReceiver -> re-arms pending reminders after
             a reboot or an app update, which is what RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED above
             is declared for. A 30-day cycle will cross at least one of those. -->
        <receiver
            android:exported="false"
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ScheduledNotificationReceiver" />
        <receiver
            android:exported="false"
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ScheduledNotificationBootReceiver">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED"/>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED"/>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON" />
                <action android:name="com.htc.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
